{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1084718-2e-prime","id":1084718,"title":"2E Prime","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-04-27T15:12:17-07:00","id":1084718,"title":"2E Prime","content":"I have a bad habit of changing topic with disregard to OP which ends in derailing threads or being ignored(second option is probably better). So this time I'm making a proper one.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1083632-what-can-prime-dispell-or-interact-with?p=1083737#post1083737\">Here</a> we talked about Dispelations and <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12061-museigen\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12061\" data-vbnamecard=\"12061\">Museigen</a> mentioned that Supernal Dispelation should be a Ruling spell. While I'm not sure it is enough there is a strong case for it:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Ruling spells grant fuller control over phenomena than a<br /> mere Compelling spell. Water can be made to flow uphill<br /> or into unnatural shapes (Matter), animals (or even human<br /> beings) can be commanded (Life or Mind), or time can be<br /> momentarily made to accelerate or slow down (Time). A<br /> Ruling spell can\u2019t fundamentally alter its target\u2019s abilities:<br /> Water can be directed, but not turned solid or gaseous.<br /> Time can be altered, but not overwritten. An animal can<br /> be commanded, but not made stronger or fiercer. </div> </div> </div>But cancelling a spell is fundamental part of the spell. I can be canceled.<br /> <br /> Thre is so many things that are wrong here that I don't know where to start.<br /> Firstly, Dispelation are almost directly ported into 2E with conjunctional arcana at first dot and none for fourth dot. Why the conjunctionals? If you want to influence magic with Prime you shouldn't need any of that, its in your purview. Mind doesn't need Spirit to work on spirits. It's a relic from 1E.<br /> If Compelling can DESTROY! the spell completely what could Ruling or Fraying do?<br /> S.Disp. description with all this 'casting into abyss' seems to be a huge overkill.<br /> <br /> PS: I'm really afraid that SoS will have plenty of conjunctional spells that will throw 2E back on 1E and we are just lucky now that they haven't got enough word count and decided to publish mostly standalone working spells.<br /> <br /> <b>Edit:</b> Even the book contradicts itself with Universal Counterspell Attainmant (2nd dot). You can affect <i>any </i>Arcanum with Prime at this level.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1090276-2e-moinen-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">[2E] Moinen's Homebrew Hub</a></b>","edited":{"userid":1747,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1747-moinen","user":"Moinen","date":". 04-27-2017, 06:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1747-moinen","name":"Moinen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1747_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"889"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-27T15:45:31-07:00","id":1084736,"title":"","content":"You also keep assuming that &quot;there are fewer spells that require conjunctional Arcana to target particular phenomena&quot; means &quot;there should be no target-based conjunctional Arcana whatsoever.&quot; A spirit's mind is a mind, however alien, but that doesn't extend to all potential overlaps. If you can't stand the thought of Awakened magic running on more stringent metrics than spell factors and raw semantics, you may have to resign yourself to the fact that that's going to continue to be a thing.<br /> <br /> I've pointed it out before and I'll point it out again: Dispellation got easier in 2e at the cost of having the default state of the effect be suppression rather than termination. Spells can end, but they don't then naturally arise again with undiminished qualities.<br /> <br /> Compelling the flaws in a spell that allow Phenomenal rules to reassert themselves to suppress it requires knowledge of the principles that that spell interferes with; Unraveling an active spell into an inactive spell just requires the capacity to target the spell itself as an extension of its caster.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-27T15:46:41-07:00","id":1084737,"title":"","content":"Sorry im confused as to what you are talking about or asking im afraid.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-27T16:31:12-07:00","id":1084753,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1084718-2e-prime?p=1084736#post1084736\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Spells can end, but they don't then naturally arise again with undiminished qualities.</div> </div> </div> </div>Doesn't that support the OP's argument? If spells can't naturally rise again with undiminished qualities, then why can you compel them to do that? Shouldn't it be a Weaving spell? If spells can naturally end, then why can't you use a Ruling spell to end them?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":191,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/191-strill","user":"Strill","date":". 04-27-2017, 07:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/191-strill","name":"Strill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-27T16:37:20-07:00","id":1084758,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1084718-2e-prime?p=1084736#post1084736\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You also keep assuming that &quot;there are fewer spells that require conjunctional Arcana to target particular phenomena&quot; means &quot;there should be no target-based conjunctional Arcana whatsoever.&quot; A spirit's mind is a mind, however alien, but that doesn't extend to all potential overlaps. If you can't stand the thought of Awakened magic running on more stringent metrics than spell factors and raw semantics, you may have to resign yourself to the fact that that's going to continue to be a thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>I prefer to use C.Thaum. and understanding <i>whys </i>of the system is very important for the game flow. With literally no rules about conjunctional spells and those in the core book being inconsistent(which have been pointed out on this forum multiple times) I'm having a hard time to find principles here beyond 'I dont' know. Port it from 1E' or 'Maybe here?'.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I've pointed it out before and I'll point it out again: Dispellation got easier in 2e at the cost of having the default state of the effect be suppression rather than termination. </div> </div> </div>The only thing that changed is withstand and Added Reach mechanic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Spells can end, but they don't then naturally arise again with undiminished qualities. </div> </div> </div>I don't know what this refers to but seems like a counter argument for your stance.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Compelling the flaws in a spell that allow Phenomenal rules to reassert themselves to suppress it requires knowledge of the principles that that spell interferes with; Unraveling an active spell into an inactive spell just requires the capacity to target the spell itself as an extension of its caster. </div> </div> </div>Why Unraveling? Prime 2nd dot Attainment can counterspell <i>any </i>spell. It all feels like artificial and unnecessary speed bump like Base, Median and Advanced Life from 1E. Also targeting the spell itself needs just a Prime Sight, 1st dot.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>totalgit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1084718-2e-prime?p=1084737#post1084737\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sorry im confused as to what you are talking about or asking im afraid.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why it works how it works? What are the hidden principles of it?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1090276-2e-moinen-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">[2E] Moinen's Homebrew Hub</a></b>","edited":{"userid":1747,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1747-moinen","user":"Moinen","date":". 04-27-2017, 07:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1747-moinen","name":"Moinen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1747_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"889"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-27T16:55:06-07:00","id":1084763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Moinen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1084718-2e-prime?p=1084758#post1084758\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The only thing that changed is withstand and Added Reach mechanic.</div> </div> </div> </div>The &quot;Added Reach mechanic&quot; is <i>how the spell worked in 1e.</i> 2e changed the thing that needed to be overcome from Potency to a semifixed Withstand rating and removed the Mana cost inherent to the default effect.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't know what this refers to but seems like a counter argument for your stance. </div> </div> </div>A spell whose effects are Withstood can be temporarily negated by raising the Withstand rating that mitigates its effects; Wards and Signs does the same thing by Shielding the target of a spell, and the spell in question still comes back when the added Withstand goes away.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Prime 2nd dot Attainment can counterspell <i>any </i>spell. </div> </div> </div>Counterspelling only words at the point of casting; that's the point of it being a distinct thing from dispellation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also targeting the spell itself needs just a Prime Sight, 1st dot. </div> </div> </div>Targeting a spell is not the same thing as negating a spell's effects any more than being able to detect a living being is the same as being able to snuff out its life.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-27T19:24:19-07:00","id":1084822,"title":"","content":"Okay - So looking at the two. I think that the real question is - why is it that dispellation can be compelled so long as you have a dot in each arcana, and yet it requires unravelling in order to perform the same feat without that same Arcana.<br /> <br /> So - the understanding of Dispel magic is that all Awakened spells contain the capacity to end and for the Fallen World's laws to reassert themselves - You compel the already existing flaws in a spell in such a way that the spell ends prematurely or is suppressed<br /> <br /> Supernal Dispellation goes a step beyond this, attacking and tearing a spell apart. In all mechanical ways, it is identical - with a single exception of the fact that you do not need to apply additional Arcana in order to affect the spell in question.<br /> <br /> Honestly, I'm not going to say that I believe that Supernal Dispellation would fit the Ruling Practice - I don't think it fits. The thing is I don't necessarily think the explanation of 'compelling the flaws' works well for Dispel Magic either. It feels like that explanation works better if it were a fraying practice than if it were a compelling practice.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MtA p. 123</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Fraying spells degrade things, weakening them and enhancing their flaws. Fraying spells can weaken targets under the Arcanum\u2019s purview: damping a fire (Forces), sapping Strength (Life), or eroding the barrier between worlds (Death, Spirit, or others, depending on the worlds in question).</div> </div> </div> </div>Just my thoughts on the subject.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1513-jakondite","name":"Jakondite","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"413"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-27T19:49:59-07:00","id":1084827,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jakondite</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1084718-2e-prime?p=1084822#post1084822\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly, I'm not going to say that I believe that Supernal Dispellation would fit the Ruling Practice - I don't think it fits. The thing is I don't necessarily think the explanation of 'compelling the flaws' works well for Dispel Magic either. It feels like that explanation works better if it were a fraying practice than if it were a compelling practice.</div> </div> </div> </div>My operating rationale is that regular Dispel Magic works by stifling the spell's interface with the world, whereas Supernal Dispellation attacks the core Truth of the spell's manifestation \u2014 one is subtler and finer-detailed work than the other and therefore needs to engage with other aspects of the Tapestry, while the other is attacking the extension of the target's caster's will that says &quot;there is a spell here,&quot; which is a more forceful change than can be managed with the Practice of Compelling.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-27T23:56:51-07:00","id":1084907,"title":"","content":"*nods* Honestly, its really the wording that throws me - because it seems like it's almost parallel to the fraying practice. Keep in mind - I like the dispellations the way they are; I think it creates an interesting balance and that because of the way it's set up, -both- supernal dispellation and dispel magic will almost always be useful (and in fact, supernal dispellation will lose it's usefulness if a mage studies all the arcana). I just think that it could have been described differently if it was to be compelling.<br /> <br /> For instance, if you described it as separating the components of the spell, I could see that as compelling. It'd be compelling the Arcanum used in one direction, and the (truth? supernal? Magic? Insert Prime Purview here) - kind of like if you influenced fire and compelled air, you could effectively increase or decrease the size and intensity of a flame (even though that would usually be Forces 2)<br /> <br /> Off topic, I suddenly have the urge to create a bunch of conjunctional effects utilizing level 1 magic to see how many higher level spells I could mimic with just compelling using that kind of logic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1513-jakondite","name":"Jakondite","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"413"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-28T04:09:47-07:00","id":1084950,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1084718-2e-prime?p=1084763#post1084763\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A spell whose effects are Withstood can be temporarily negated by raising the Withstand rating that mitigates its effects; Wards and Signs does the same thing by Shielding the target of a spell, and the spell in question still comes back when the added Withstand goes away.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except that this particular Compelling spell destroys spells in a Lasting way. Spells, as you said can't come from dead so either the effect is extremely potent or all magic is extremely fragile.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Counterspelling only words at the point of casting; that's the point of it being a distinct thing from dispellation. </div> </div> </div>Im trying to find some consistency and reasoning that would match practices and purviews, not just arbitral statements 'this is hard, make it Unraveling but work like Compelling spell you ported before. did I mention that I like how I love that it uses mechanics of Unmaking spells with this kind of withstand?'<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Targeting a spell is not the same thing as negating a spell's effects any more than being able to detect a living being is the same as being able to snuff out its life. </div> </div> </div>Again. That means Dispelation shouldn't be 1 dot spell. I would rather combine both dispelations and make them Fraying so they wouldn't break rules.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jakondite</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1084718-2e-prime?p=1084822#post1084822\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just my thoughts on the subject.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would be fine with Dispelation as Fraying and some room to toy with spells at lower practices. Just like you dont need to Fray minds to use Psychic Domination on them.<br /> 1E had a Controled Dispellation (3rd dot) spell that worked like the Fate conjunctional of 2E Dispelation which exempts targets. I would make it a Ruling spell now.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1084718-2e-prime?p=1084827#post1084827\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My operating rationale is that regular Dispel Magic works by stifling the spell's interface with the world, whereas Supernal Dispellation attacks the core Truth of the spell's manifestation \u2014 one is subtler and finer-detailed work than the other and therefore needs to engage with other aspects of the Tapestry, while the other is attacking the extension of the target's caster's will that says &quot;there is a spell here,&quot; which is a more forceful change than can be managed with the Practice of Compelling.</div> </div> </div> </div>But the disparity of levels on which you achieve basically the same effect has no equivalent in other practices. To attack you just need Fraying practice or command it with Ruling to just go rogue.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jakondite</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1084718-2e-prime?p=1084907#post1084907\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For instance, if you described it as separating the components of the spell, I could see that as compelling. It'd be compelling the Arcanum used in one direction, and the (truth? supernal? Magic? Insert Prime Purview here) - kind of like if you influenced fire and compelled air, you could effectively increase or decrease the size and intensity of a flame (even though that would usually be Forces 2)<br /> <br /> Off topic, I suddenly have the urge to create a bunch of conjunctional effects utilizing level 1 magic to see how many higher level spells I could mimic with just compelling using that kind of logic.</div> </div> </div> </div>Interesting theory. I would like to see them but would this principle extend to higher practices? It looks like you could omit the combined casting by having both effects act on one goal. Cast multiple unveilings at one target with a single spell to examine it; help someone swim faster with Life/Matter 3 Weaving spell that changes your hands and legs into flippers <i>and </i>weaves the water around you to aid you even further, achieving effects of the magnified force you would expect from Patterning spell?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1090276-2e-moinen-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">[2E] Moinen's Homebrew Hub</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1747-moinen","name":"Moinen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1747_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"889"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-28T08:47:58-07:00","id":1085050,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jakondite</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1084718-2e-prime?p=1084907#post1084907\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Keep in mind - I like the dispellations the way they are; I think it creates an interesting balance and that because of the way it's set up, -both- supernal dispellation and dispel magic will almost always be useful (and in fact, supernal dispellation will lose it's usefulness if a mage studies all the arcana).</div> </div> </div> </div>Interestingly enough, this is one of those cases where the way Imbued Items work comes in handy; Supernal Dispellation is easier to Imbue than an omnilateral Dispel Magic and easier for anyone who isn't an Adept or Master of Prime to wield.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Moinen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1084718-2e-prime?p=1084950#post1084950\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Except that this particular Compelling spell destroys spells in a Lasting way.</div> </div> </div> </div>To repeat: The default effect of Dispel Magic in 2e is &quot;the target spell is suppressed for the Duration of Dispel Magic.&quot; In order to completely destroy a spell with Dispel Magic, you need to use the Reach effect; without assuming the use of a rote, you hit the point of being able to destroy a spell as a Lasting effect with an instant action sans-Paradox with Prime 3 and the rudiments of the component Arcana. Contrariwise, Supernal Dispellation requires the full extent of a Master's free Reach but works on everything short of archmastery without generalized knowledge of everything for the purposes of making a bit of magic go away for a scene.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> did I mention that I like how I love that it uses mechanics of Unmaking spells with this kind of withstand? </div> </div> </div>You don't need Unmaking to make somebody lose a finger for an hour.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Again. That means Dispelation shouldn't be 1 dot spell. I would rather combine both dispelations and make them Fraying so they wouldn't break rules. </div> </div> </div>The rules that include &quot;accomplishing an effect at a lower dot level with different Arcanum is allowed&quot;?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But the disparity of levels on which you achieve basically the same effect has no equivalent in other practices. To attack you just need Fraying practice or command it with Ruling to just go rogue. </div> </div> </div>Beating back an exercise of supernatural influence has precedent as an Unraveling effect via the Banishment analogues in Death and Spirit. A spell does not rage out of control after casting while it is on the caster's spell control and it is not within the domain of the Traveler's Prime Arcanum to so directly subvert the will of another mage.<br /> <br /> There being few similar effects as far apart in Practice as the Dispellations does not mean there is no equivalent \u2014 removing a mage's soul requires Unmaking the connection to their Gnosis that safeguards it rather than Fraying the normal containment process that holds in a Sleeper's soul, and there are memory-wiping effects at both Mind 2 and Mind 5 just as there are sympathy-negaters at Space 2 and 5.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-28T12:27:31-07:00","id":1085098,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1084718-2e-prime?p=1085050#post1085050\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Interestingly enough, this is one of those cases where the way Imbued Items work comes in handy; Supernal Dispellation is easier to Imbue than an omnilateral Dispel Magic and easier for anyone who isn't an Adept or Master of Prime to wield.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed. So someone who had both would likely focu son Dispel Magic if they had to personally deal with something, but Supernal Dispellation would be their likely go-to ability for what they grant their students, sleepwalkers, etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Moinen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1084718-2e-prime?p=1084950#post1084950\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Interesting theory. I would like to see them but would this principle extend to higher practices? It looks like you could omit the combined casting by having both effects act on one goal. Cast multiple unveilings at one target with a single spell to examine it; help someone swim faster with Life/Matter 3 Weaving spell that changes your hands and legs into flippers <i>and </i>weaves the water around you to aid you even further, achieving effects of the magnified force you would expect from Patterning spell?</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't see why not? That's basically being fairly creative about your magic to create similar, more powerful effects. You heal a person and accelerate that healing (life 3) why should you not be able to replicate a powerful life 4 effect with that? It doesn't lessen the worth of having Life 4 but does heighten the worth of taking in multiple arcana in the hopes of recreating more powerful effects.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1513-jakondite","name":"Jakondite","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"413"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}